# tools/ca/caravan_edge_witness.rish -- the three refusals a request earns before anything is touched, and the order Caravan chose.
#
# WHY THIS RUNG. `caravan/refusals.rye` stated the eleven answers a capability invocation may receive and named, for each
# of the seven it could not answer, the subsystem that owes it. Three of those seven were always one family, and the
# agenda said so in its own debt reasons: argument checking owes `seL4_InvalidArgument`, bounds checking owes
# `seL4_RangeError`, alignment checking owes `seL4_AlignmentError`. They are one family because they are one MOMENT --
# each is a question asked of the request alone, answerable before a slot is touched, a lineage walked, or a byte spent.
# `caravan/edge.rye` is that moment, and it closes the family in a single rung.
#
# WHAT IS PROVEN HERE, each on metal.
#
#   EACH CHECK REFUSES ALONE, on a request that fails only it. A check proven only in company is a check that might
#   never have run at all.
#
#   THE ORDER IS REAL AND IT IS CARAVAN'S OWN. seL4 lists a method's errors ALPHABETICALLY in its interface XML --
#   DeleteFirst, FailedLookup, IllegalOperation, InvalidArgument, InvalidCapability, NotEnoughMemory, RangeError -- which
#   is a reading order rather than a checking order, measured here rather than assumed. So Caravan states its own, and
#   states why: each check depends only on facts the checks before it established. The module proves the order by
#   building requests that fail two and three checks at once, including one that fails the first and third while the
#   second passes -- so the answer is genuinely the earliest rather than the first found by luck of adjacency.
#
#   EXACTLY ONE ANSWER, SWEPT. Over every kind, eleven widths, five counts spanning the kernel's fan-out limit, and a
#   thousand-odd addresses, every request is either answered clean with no check failing, or refused by the answer
#   belonging to its earliest failing check. Both arms are exercised: the sweep holds welcomed and refused requests
#   alike, asserted rather than hoped.
#
#   THE SEAM WITH THE REGION BELOW, AND THE TWO LINES KEPT APART. Two conditions `untyped.rye` refuses by named Rye
#   error have published kernel answers, and the edge speaks them without the region changing at all. Meanwhile the
#   kernel creates at most 256 objects in one invocation and Caravan's region carves at most 64: a request for 65 is
#   WELCOMED by the edge, because the kernel permits it, and REFUSED by the region, because we do not hold that much.
#   Both are honest, and the rung proves each names its own author rather than speaking for the other.
#
#   THE THREE SENTENCES ARE seL4's OWN, RE-READ FROM THE VENDORED BSD-2-Clause XML on every run. The published
#   descriptions this module was written to are asserted still standing, so a reword upstream reds the rung rather than
#   leaving our reading quietly orphaned -- REDS %107's two-sided lesson, kept two rungs on.
#
#   THE FAN-OUT LIMIT IS STATED TWICE UPSTREAM AND READ BOTH WAYS. seL4 gives 256 as `KernelRetypeFanOutLimit`'s CMake
#   default and again as the fallback in `types.h` when the config macro is absent. The witness reads both, so a bump
#   to either is caught even if the other lags.
#
# WHAT IS NOT PROVEN. No kernel booted and no invocation crossed a real boundary. This is policy on hosted ground: the
# checks are ours, written to the shape of the answers seL4 publishes, and they earn their behavioural proof the day a
# root task actually invokes.
#
# Season: the Lindy-priority Microkernel Target double-seat (seated `20260817`, ordered ahead of Seasons A-H),
# Equinox 1 -- Caravan on the microkernel. Kin: tools/ca/caravan_refusals_witness.rish (the agenda this closes three rows
# of) - tools/ca/caravan_untyped_witness.rish (the region this seams with) - tools/ca/caravan_ipc_buffer_witness.rish -
# tools/ca/caravan_derivation_witness.rish -
# external-research/20260821-043831_the-libsel4-reach-and-the-vocabulary-caravan-inherits.md
#
# Run from the repository root:
#   rishi/bin/rishi run tools/ca/caravan_edge_witness.rish

say "caravan-edge: Language EN -- three questions asked of a request alone are one family, because they are one moment: before a slot is touched, a lineage walked, or a byte spent."
say "caravan-edge: Style Radiant -- four claims in order. Each check refuses alone. The order is Caravan's own and says so. Exactly one answer comes back. And the region below keeps its own line."
say "caravan-edge: Lens TAME -- the module's asserts are the specification, and all three RED paths are proven by planting them: a reversed order, two checks collapsed into one answer, and an alignment check that welcomes every address."

let zig = "vendor/zig-toolchain/zig"
let bin = "caravan/bin/edge"
let errors = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/include/sel4/errors.h"
let api = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/include/interfaces/object-api.xml"
let riscv_api = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/arch_include/riscv/interfaces/object-api-arch.xml"
let types = "vendor/sel4/libsel4/include/sel4/types.h"
let config = "vendor/sel4/config.cmake"
let control = "tools/fixtures/caravan_edge_control.sh"
let build_dir = "tools/.build/caravan_edge"

let mk = run ["sh" "-c" "mkdir -p caravan/bin ${build_dir}"]
assert mk.ok else "caravan-edge: could not make the build directories"

# 0. PRECONDITION, an environment fact rather than a tree red: the gitlink must actually be checked out, since the
#    vocabulary, the three published sentences, and the fan-out limit are all read from seL4's own files.
let checked_out = run ["sh" "-c" "test -f ${api} && test -f ${riscv_api} && test -f ${types} && test -f ${config} && echo yes || echo no"]
assert (trim checked_out.out) == "yes" else "caravan-edge: vendor/sel4 is not checked out -- run `git submodule update --init --recursive` first; this is an environment precondition, not a tree red"

# 1. THE MODULE BUILDS AND PROVES ITSELF. Its asserts are the specification.
let build = run ["sh" "-c" "env RYE_ZIG=${zig} rye/bin/rye build caravan/edge.rye -femit-bin=${bin}"]
assert build.ok else "caravan-edge: the module did not build"

let test = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} selftest 2>&1"]
assert test.ok else "caravan-edge: the self-test exited non-zero"
assert test.out contains "GREEN" else "caravan-edge: the self-test is not GREEN"
assert test.out contains "before a slot is touched or a byte is spent" else "caravan-edge: the one-moment claim was not made"
assert test.out contains "answers seL4_InvalidArgument" else "caravan-edge: the argument debt is not answered by seL4_InvalidArgument"
assert test.out contains "answers seL4_RangeError" else "caravan-edge: the bounds debt is not answered by seL4_RangeError"
assert test.out contains "answers seL4_AlignmentError" else "caravan-edge: the alignment debt is not answered by seL4_AlignmentError"
assert test.out contains "the order is Caravan's own" else "caravan-edge: the order is no longer named as our own design judgement"
assert test.out contains "seL4 lists a method's errors alphabetically" else "caravan-edge: the measured reason the order is ours was dropped"
assert test.out contains "the earliest wins" else "caravan-edge: the earliest-answer rule was not claimed"
assert test.out contains "at most 256 objects in one invocation" else "caravan-edge: the kernel's fan-out line moved without the witness moving with it"
assert test.out contains "region carves at most 64 -- two lines, kept apart" else "caravan-edge: the two lines are no longer held apart"

# 2. THE ORDER IS PRINTED WITH ITS REASONS, so a reader meets the design judgement rather than a bare sequence.
let table = run ["sh" "-c" "${bin} table > ${build_dir}/table.txt 2>&1; echo done"]
assert (trim table.out) == "done" else "caravan-edge: the worked request did not print"

let printed = run ["sh" "-c" "cat ${build_dir}/table.txt"]
assert printed.out contains "check 1 argument answers seL4_InvalidArgument" else "caravan-edge: argument no longer stands first in the order"
assert printed.out contains "check 2 range answers seL4_RangeError" else "caravan-edge: range no longer stands second in the order"
assert printed.out contains "check 3 alignment answers seL4_AlignmentError" else "caravan-edge: alignment no longer stands last in the order"
assert printed.out contains "the other two questions have no subject" else "caravan-edge: the reason argument goes first was dropped"
assert printed.out contains "needs no address at all" else "caravan-edge: the reason range goes second was dropped"
assert printed.out contains "an address the caller supplies" else "caravan-edge: the reason alignment goes last was dropped"
assert printed.out contains "a request failing 3 checks answers seL4_InvalidArgument -- the earliest, never the loudest" else "caravan-edge: a request failing all three was not answered by the earliest check"
assert printed.out contains "a request passing all three answers seL4_NoError" else "caravan-edge: a clean request was not welcomed"

# 3. THE THREE REFUSALS ARE THE KERNEL'S OWN, each stated once in the vendored errors header.
let named_argument = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'seL4_InvalidArgument' ${errors}"]
assert (trim named_argument.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4_InvalidArgument is no longer stated once in the vendored errors.h"

let named_range = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'seL4_RangeError' ${errors}"]
assert (trim named_range.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4_RangeError is no longer stated once in the vendored errors.h"

let named_alignment = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'seL4_AlignmentError' ${errors}"]
assert (trim named_alignment.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4_AlignmentError is no longer stated once in the vendored errors.h"

# 4. THE THREE SENTENCES THIS MODULE WAS WRITTEN TO ARE STILL PUBLISHED, read from the vendored XML. This is the
#    two-sided binding REDS %107 taught: a reword upstream must red the rung rather than orphan our reading in silence.
let says_argument = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'is too big or too small for the requested object type' ${api}"]
assert (trim says_argument.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4 no longer describes seL4_InvalidArgument as a size too big or too small for the object type -- re-read it and move our check with it"

let says_range = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'CONFIG_RETYPE_FAN_OUT_LIMIT' ${api}"]
assert (trim says_range.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4 no longer describes seL4_RangeError against CONFIG_RETYPE_FAN_OUT_LIMIT -- re-read it and move our check with it"

let says_alignment = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'is not aligned to the page size of' ${riscv_api}"]
assert (trim says_alignment.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: riscv seL4 no longer describes seL4_AlignmentError as a vaddr unaligned to the page size -- re-read it and move our check with it"

let api_licence = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'SPDX-License-Identifier: BSD-2-Clause' ${api}"]
assert (trim api_licence.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: the interface XML is no longer tagged BSD-2-Clause -- the per-file sweep governs what we may read"

# 5. THE ORDER IS OURS BECAUSE SEL4 PUBLISHES NONE, and that is measured rather than asserted. Retype's own error block
#    stands in strict alphabetical order, which is a reading order and cannot be a checking order.
let alphabetical = run ["sh" "tools/fixtures/caravan_edge_order_scan.sh"]
assert alphabetical.ok else "caravan-edge: the order scan did not run -- ${alphabetical.out}"
assert alphabetical.out contains "errors=7" else "caravan-edge: Retype no longer publishes seven errors -- re-read the block before trusting either order"
assert alphabetical.out contains "verdict=alphabetical" else "caravan-edge: seL4 no longer lists Retype's errors alphabetically -- if it now publishes a check sequence, read it and weigh ours against it rather than keeping ours by default"
say "caravan-edge: seL4's own Retype error block -- ${trim alphabetical.out}"

# 6. THE FAN-OUT LIMIT IS 256, stated two independent ways upstream and read both ways here.
let fan_out_types = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -c 'seL4_UntypedRetypeMaxObjects 256' ${types}"]
assert (trim fan_out_types.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: seL4_UntypedRetypeMaxObjects no longer falls back to 256 in the vendored types.h -- retype_fan_out_limit must move with it"

let fan_out_config = run ["sh" "-c" "grep -A2 'KernelRetypeFanOutLimit RETYPE_FAN_OUT_LIMIT' ${config} | grep -c 'DEFAULT 256'"]
assert (trim fan_out_config.out) == "1" else "caravan-edge: KernelRetypeFanOutLimit no longer defaults to 256 in the vendored config -- retype_fan_out_limit must move with it"

# 7. RED PATH ONE -- the order lives in exactly one list, because it is a design judgement rather than something seL4
#    publishes. Reversed, a request failing the first and third checks is answered by the wrong one.
let order = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} order 2>/dev/null"]
assert order.ok else "caravan-edge: the order control did not run"
assert order.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-edge: the reversed-order copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert order.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-edge: RED path one did not fire -- a reversed checking order passed the self-test"

# 8. RED PATH TWO -- an edge that collapses two of its three answers tells a caller to fix the wrong argument, which is
#    the same fault the refusal vocabulary was built to make impossible one rung down.
let collapse = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} collapse 2>/dev/null"]
assert collapse.ok else "caravan-edge: the collapse control did not run"
assert collapse.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-edge: the collapsed-answer copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert collapse.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-edge: RED path two did not fire -- two checks answering as one passed the self-test"

# 9. RED PATH THREE -- an alignment check that welcomes every address is the quietest of the three failures, since
#    nothing refuses and every request looks healthy. The second movement asks each check to refuse alone.
let alignment = run ["sh" "-c" "sh ${control} ${zig} ${build_dir} alignment 2>/dev/null"]
assert alignment.ok else "caravan-edge: the alignment control did not run"
assert alignment.out contains "planted=1" else "caravan-edge: the always-aligned copy was not planted -- the RED path would have proven nothing"
assert alignment.out contains "verdict=refused" else "caravan-edge: RED path three did not fire -- an alignment check welcoming every address passed the self-test"

say "caravan-edge: three questions asked of the request alone close the whole edge family in one rung -- a size a kind cannot be, a count past the kernel's line, an address off its own boundary."
say "caravan-edge: each check refuses alone on a request that fails only it, and exactly one answer comes back across a swept space holding both welcomed and refused requests."
say "caravan-edge: the order is Caravan's own and the module says so, because seL4 lists Retype's errors alphabetically -- proven alphabetical on this run rather than taken on impression."
say "caravan-edge: the kernel's 256 and Caravan's 64 stay two lines, so a request for sixty-five is welcomed by the edge and refused by the region, each naming its own author."
say "GREEN: Caravan checks its edges, and the last family on the refusal agenda is answered."
